## 15. Types of Linked Lists

There are three important types:

### 1. Singly Linked List

```text
10 → 20 → 30 → None
```

Each node points only to the next node.

### 2. Doubly Linked List

```text
None ← 10 ⇄ 20 ⇄ 30 → None
```

Each node has:

```text
previous
next
```

### 3. Circular Linked List

The last node points back to the first node.

```text
10 → 20 → 30
↑         ↓
└─────────┘
```

We will learn these separately.

---

## 16. Important Terminology

| Term      | Meaning                    |
| --------- | -------------------------- |
| Node      | Stores data and a link     |
| Data      | Value stored in a node     |
| Next      | Reference to the next node |
| Head      | First node                 |
| Tail      | Last node                  |
| Traversal | Visiting nodes one by one  |
| `None`    | No next node               |

---

## 17. Complexity

For a basic singly linked list:

| Operation                  | Time |
| -------------------------- | ---: |
| Access by index            | O(N) |
| Search                     | O(N) |
| Traverse                   | O(N) |
| Insert at beginning        | O(1) |
| Insert at end without tail | O(N) |

If a linked list maintains a `tail` reference, inserting at the end can be **O(1)**.

